14:50:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Paper straws lose strength when their paper fibers become wet. Dry paper is stiff because its cellulose fibers are tightly bonded together; water disrupts those bonds, makes the fibers swell, and can cause the layers of the straw to separate. The mouth end fails first because it gets a particularly bad combination of conditions: - **Saliva and warm liquid wet it continuously.** Saliva contains salts, proteins, and mild surfactants that can help liquid penetrate coatings and paper layers. Warmth also speeds wetting. - **It is mechanically stressed.** Your lips squeeze it, teeth may bite it, and suction creates pressure differences and repeated flexing. Wet paper that is repeatedly squeezed or bent quickly buckles and frays. - **There is a wet/dry boundary.** Liquid can wick into the paper from the mouth end, creating uneven swelling. That promotes delamination and collapse at one localized spot. - **Abrasion matters.** Contact with lips, teeth, ice, and movement in the drink rubs away or damages the straw’s protective coating. When a straw is simply submerged, it may remain intact for a while because it is not being pinched, sucked on, bent, or abraded. Its coating may also slow water penetration, and any softening may be more uniform rather than concentrated at one stressed point. Given enough time, however—especially in a hot drink—most paper straws will eventually soften and break down while fully submerged too.
