14:50:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right: water and saliva both wet the paper, and simple wetting alone is not enough to explain the difference. The main difference is **mechanical loading and pressure**, not that saliva somehow uniquely dissolves the straw. When the straw is fully submerged and open at both ends: - Water is inside and outside the tube. - The pressure is nearly equal on both sides of the wall. - The softened paper may have lost stiffness, but it is not being strongly squeezed or bent, so it can still look structurally intact. At the mouth end while drinking: - You create lower pressure inside the straw by sucking. - Atmospheric pressure outside the straw then pushes inward on its softened walls. - Your lips also pinch the tube, and teeth or mouth movement may bend it. - Once wet paper has softened, that inward pressure and bending can make the circular tube buckle, flatten, or delaminate. So “submerged” does not mean the straw is still strong—it may be just as waterlogged. It simply has much less reason to collapse because it is pressure-balanced and not being squeezed.
